showcase.question.long.title=Choose how the agent should migrate a compatibility-sensitive subsystem with several downstream consumers
showcase.question.long.message=The selected strategy will be applied across backend services, frontend clients, persisted session history, and older peers that may omit recently added fields. Each service reads and writes the shared event log, so a change to the wire format reaches every consumer at once. Some consumers run an older build and still expect the previous field layout. Others run a newer build and already write the extended layout. A remote frontend replays the whole history on reconnect, so a strategy that breaks an old record breaks the reconnect too. The migration must therefore keep every stored record readable while both layouts are in use. It must also let a half-upgraded fleet keep working until the last peer upgrades. Consider how each option handles a record written before the migration, a record written during the migration, and a record written after it. Consider the extra code each option leaves behind, and how long that code must stay. Consider what happens when one participant disconnects in the middle of the rollout and reconnects against a changed format. Weigh the rollout safety, the implementation cost, the replay compatibility, and the recovery path before you choose.
showcase.question.long.label=Which migration strategy should be used while keeping mixed-version sessions readable and preventing partially upgraded clients from losing state?
showcase.question.long.description=Compare rollout safety, implementation complexity, replay compatibility, and the ability to recover when one participant disconnects halfway through the migration.
showcase.question.long.option.incremental=Incremental rollout\nIntroduce the new representation behind compatibility adapters, retain the old read path temporarily, and remove it only after all supported peers have upgraded.
showcase.question.long.option.compatibility=Permanent compatibility layer\nKeep both representations indefinitely and normalize them at every boundary where events are persisted, replayed, or sent to a remote frontend.
showcase.question.long.option.replacement=Immediate replacement\nSwitch every producer and consumer in one release, reject older payloads explicitly, and keep the implementation smaller at the cost of mixed-version compatibility.
